        </div>
        <div class="dm-help">$1,150 ÷ 21 = $54.76 a day. Mark rent paid to release the bill reserve.</div>
      </DocMock>
    </div>

    <h2 id="sts-mood">How the color states work</h2>
    <p>
      The Safe to Spend number changes color based on the ratio of today's daily rate to your
      <em>average</em> daily budget (period budget ÷ total days). The thresholds are relative, not
      fixed dollar amounts, which means the same number can be green for one user and yellow for
      another, depending on their budget size.
    </p>
    <ul class="doc-status-list">
      <li><span class="status-dot green"></span><strong>Comfortable.</strong> Daily rate ≥ 80% of average daily budget. Spend confidently.</li>
      <li><span class="status-dot yellow"></span><strong>Moderate.</strong> Daily rate between 50% and 80%. Skip the impulse buys for now.</li>
      <li><span class="status-dot orange"></span><strong>Tight.</strong> Daily rate is positive but under 50%. Limit discretionary purchases for the rest of the period.</li>
      <li><span class="status-dot red"></span><strong>Overcommitted.</strong> Daily rate is zero or negative. Remaining budget is fully spoken for, or you've overspent.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why a daily rate instead of a total remaining?</h3>
    <p>
      Showing a total creates a psychological trap. If you have $600 left and 15 days to go, $600
      feels like a lot. But it's only $40 a day. The daily rate makes the constraint concrete and
      actionable, when dinner is $50 and Safe to Spend is $38, the decision is obvious.
    </p>
    <p>
      The daily rate also self-adjusts as the period progresses. Underspend on Monday and Tuesday's
      number ticks up. Overspend and it ticks down. The number reflects the current moment, not
      just a snapshot of what you started with.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sts-worked-example">Worked example</h2>
    <p>Concrete numbers showing how Safe to Spend behaves across a pay period.</p>
    <div class="concept-example">
      <div class="ce-header">Setup</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Monthly pay period, budget $3,000</li>
        <li>Savings target: $600 (none contributed yet)</li>
        <li>Rent bill: $900 (red, due on the 1st, not yet paid)</li>
        <li>It's the 10th of the month (21 days remaining)</li>
        <li>Total spending so far this month: $350</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="ce-calc">
        <div class="ce-line"><span>Remaining Budget</span><span>$3,000 − $350 = $2,650</span></div>
        <div class="ce-line"><span>Bill Reserve (rent)</span><span>− $900</span></div>
        <div class="ce-line"><span>Savings Reserve</span><span>− $600</span></div>
        <div class="ce-line ce-total"><span>Spendable over 21 days</span><span>$1,150</span></div>
        <div class="ce-line ce-result"><span>Safe to Spend</span><span>$1,150 ÷ 21 = $54.76/day</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="ce-note">
        Now you pay rent and mark the bill paid. The $900 Bill Reserve releases. Safe to Spend recalculates:
      </div>
      <div class="ce-calc">
        <div class="ce-line"><span>Spendable over 21 days</span><span>$1,150 + $900 = $2,050</span></div>
        <div class="ce-line ce-result"><span>Safe to Spend after marking rent paid</span><span>$2,050 ÷ 21 = $97.62/day</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

