    <h2 id="money-pools">Money Pools</h2>
    <p>
      Most budgeting apps track one number: total spending. They treat your money as a single pool
      and subtract from it. BudgetBaby tracks four distinct pools because your money doesn't actually
      live in one place, and collapsing it hides important information.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cash.</strong> Physical cash you carry or spend. Every cash transaction reduces the
        Cash balance immediately.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Debit.</strong> Money in your bank account. Logging a Debit transaction decreases
        the Debit balance; logging income increases it. Debit is an approximation of your real
        checking account based on what you've told the app.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Credit.</strong> Purchases on a credit card. Credit spending counts toward your
        period budget (it affects Safe to Spend) but does <em>not</em> reduce Cash or Debit
        immediately. It's a deferred liability, the cash transfer happens when you pay the card.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Savings.</strong> A protected pool for goal money. Savings transactions are
        explicitly excluded from Safe to Spend so goal money isn't counted as available.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Why four pools instead of one?</h3>
    <p>
      Two people can both have $1,200 in their accounts and have completely different financial
      realities. Person A has $1,200 in Debit with no obligations due. Person B has $1,200 in Debit
      with $800 rent due tomorrow and $400 sitting on a card from last week's groceries. A
      single-balance app shows them the same number. BudgetBaby shows them different pictures.
    </p>
    <p>
      The four-pool model also handles credit cards correctly. Logging a card purchase as Credit
      shows it in your spending history and Safe to Spend, but Debit stays accurate. When you pay
      the bill, log a Debit → Credit transfer. Debit drops, Credit zeroes out, and the books match
      reality.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
