    <h2 id="priority-colors">Priority colors</h2>
    <p>
      Every category has a priority color. The color isn't decorative, it changes how BudgetBaby
      treats spending in calculations and how it appears in your Insights breakdown.
    </p>
    <ul class="doc-status-list">
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot red"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Essentials (red).</strong> Non-negotiable bills and necessities, rent, utilities,
          insurance, loan payments. Unpaid red bills are reserved out of Safe to Spend
          automatically. You physically can't see that money as available spending until you mark
          the bill paid. This is the most important behavior in the priority system.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot yellow"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Variable (yellow).</strong> Important but flexible spending, groceries, gas,
          healthcare. These are needs, not wants, but you can adjust the amount in a tight period.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot green"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Fun (green).</strong> Lifestyle and enjoyment, dining out, entertainment,
          shopping, hobbies. The first place to look when you need to cut back.
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="status-dot muted"></span>
        <div>
          <strong>Unplanned (gray).</strong> Unexpected or miscellaneous costs. Useful for one-off
          purchases, "oops" expenses, and anything you want to track but haven't categorized yet.
          Unplanned spending doesn't affect the Bill Reserve.
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Free users</strong> get 12 default categories spread across the four colors (4 red, 3
      yellow, 4 green, 1 gray) plus up to 5 custom categories.
      <strong>Premium users</strong> can create unlimited custom categories, each with its own SF
      Symbol icon, color, and name.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
