        <div class="dm-list">
          <div class="dm-row"><span class="dm-dot discretionary"></span><span class="dm-row-label">Dining Out</span><span class="dm-row-meta">$95</span></div>
          <div class="dm-row"><span class="dm-dot discretionary"></span><span class="dm-row-label">Coffee &amp; Cafe</span><span class="dm-row-meta">$32</span></div>
        </div>
      </DocMock>
    </div>

    <h2 id="bucket-names">Bucket names (renaming)</h2>
    <p>
      The four priority colors have default display names. Essentials, Variable, Fun, Unplanned, but you can rename them in <strong>Settings → Budget Settings → Bucket Names</strong>. Some
      people prefer "Bills / Lifestyle / Future" or "Must / Should / Want / Whoops." The colors and
      behaviors stay the same; only the labels change.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-tip">
      <strong>Renaming is cosmetic.</strong> The Bill Reserve still attaches to red categories
      regardless of what you call them. Renaming doesn't change priority semantics, it just makes
      the labels match how you think about your money.
    </div>

    <h2 id="period-mechanics">Budget period mechanics</h2>
    <p>
      A budget period is a fixed window matching your pay frequency. It has a start date, end
      date, budget amount, and all the transactions, bills, and pool balances that belong to it.
    </p>

    <h3>Rollover at period end</h3>
    <p>
      When a period ends, the next time you launch BudgetBaby it closes the previous period and
      opens a new one. The previous period becomes read-only and is stored in History. Rollover
      runs at app launch, if you don't open the app for a few days, the rollover happens the next
      time you do.
    </p>

    <h3>Balance carryforward</h3>
    <p>
      Cash and Debit balances carry forward automatically. If your Debit pool ended the period at
      $427.50, the new period starts with $427.50 in Debit. Money in your bank account doesn't
      disappear at the end of a pay period, it's still there, and BudgetBaby treats it that way.
    </p>
    <p>
      Credit (owed) and Savings balances are managed separately. Credit owed represents what you
      owe on your card in the current period. Savings balances are managed through goal
      contributions and transfers, not period rollover.
    </p>

    <h3>Surplus rollover (optional)</h3>
    <p>
      If you spend less than your budget in a period, the unspent surplus can roll into the next
      period as additional budget. This is configurable in Settings → Budget Settings. Surplus is
      displayed separately so you always know whether your available budget comes from regular pay
      or from a previous period's savings.
    </p>

    <h3>Bills per period</h3>
    <p>
      Bills are assigned to a period based on their due day. A rent bill due on the 1st appears in
      every period that contains the 1st. Bills aren't carried forward; each period generates its
      own occurrence from the recurring schedule. This means you never have duplicate bills, stale
      bills from last month, or bills that disappear if you don't log them in time.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
      <DocMock label="Period rollover" caption="Cash and Debit carry forward. Credit and Savings stay in their own pools.">
        <div class="dm-bar"><span class="dm-bar-title">Period summary</span><span class="dm-bar-action">Close</span></div>
