    <h2 id="budget-guardrails">Budget Guardrails</h2>
    <p>
      Some operations are mathematically impossible without breaking your budget, spending more
      than allocated, allocating more than total budget, transferring from a pool that has nothing
      in it. BudgetBaby refuses these actions instead of letting them silently corrupt the math.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Hard block.</strong> The Save button is disabled and a red error card explains why,
        including the maximum amount you could have entered. This protects against negative
        remaining budget, over-allocation, and transfers exceeding the source pool's balance.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Soft warning.</strong> The action is allowed but an orange warning card flags the
        unusual state, for example, an expense that would push a single pool below zero even when
        the overall budget can absorb it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Education.</strong> Every financial metric on the home screen has an (i) info
        icon. Tap any number you don't recognize and a sheet describes what it represents and how
        it's calculated.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On launch, BudgetBaby's data integrity service also auto-corrects any remaining-balance value
      that ended up negative on a previous version of the app (before guardrails existed). It does
      this by inserting a corrective <code>BudgetAdjustment</code> that brings the remainder back
      to zero. The correction is logged in your Activity Log so you can see exactly what was
      adjusted and why.
    </p>

