    <h2 id="why-local-first">Why local-first</h2>
    <p>
      BudgetBaby stores everything on your device by default using SwiftData. No account is needed
      to use the app. Cloud backup, cloud sync, and account features are opt-in. This is a
      deliberate design choice, not a technical limitation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Financial data is among the most sensitive data someone can have. An app that requires a
      cloud account and stores transactions on a server creates a single target for unauthorized
      access: the server. An app that keeps everything on individual hardware-encrypted devices
      distributes that risk.
    </p>
    <p>
      There's also a practical argument. Local storage means the app works without an internet
      connection, works instantly without server round-trips, and continues to work even if our
      servers are down. Your budget doesn't depend on our uptime.
    </p>
    <p>
      When you do opt into cloud backup, the data is encrypted on your device with AES-256-GCM
      before it uploads. We store an encrypted blob we can't read. The encryption key lives in your
      iOS Keychain. This isn't a compromise between privacy and convenience, it's both.
    </p>

